      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pat the duck legs dry. Heat oil in a Dutch oven over medium-high heat. Sear the duck legs skin-side down for 5 to 6 minutes until the skin is golden and a lot of fat has rendered. Turn and sear for 2 minutes on the other side. Remove and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour off most of the duck fat, leaving about 1 tbsp in the pot. Fry the shallots, garlic, ginger, and lemongrass over medium heat for 2 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mash the fermented tofu with a fork until smooth. Add it to the pot and stir for 1 minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Return the duck legs to the pot. Add the coconut water, fish sauce, and sugar. Add enough water to come halfway up the duck legs."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bring to a simmer. Cover and braise on the lowest heat for 60 to 75 minutes until the duck is very tender and falling from the bone."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the duck legs and reduce the sauce over high heat until it thickens slightly. Taste and adjust with more fish sauce if needed."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Serve the duck legs with the sauce spooned over. Garnish with spring onion and coriander. Serve with steamed rice."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The sauce tastes too pungent and fermented.",
              "cause": "Too much chao was used.",
              "fix": "Start with 2 small cubes of fermented tofu. You can always add more but cannot reduce the flavour once it is in."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The duck skin is flabby and greasy.",
              "cause": "The skin was not rendered properly before braising.",
              "fix": "Sear the duck skin-side down on medium heat long enough to render the fat. The skin should look golden and slightly shrunken."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The duck meat is still tough after an hour.",
              "cause": "The braise was simmering too vigorously.",
              "fix": "Duck legs need very gentle, slow heat. Keep the lid on and the heat as low as possible. Check at 75 minutes."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Vịt nấu chao comes from the Mekong Delta in southern Vietnam, where duck is a staple protein. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The use of fermented tofu in a braise shows the influence of Chinese cooking in the region. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Duck farming is common along the many canals and waterways of the delta. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This dish is considered a special-occasion meal and is served at family celebrations. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Outside the Mekong Delta, it is less well known but highly regarded by those who know it."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
